1
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:11,740
This is the first part of a two-part module covering an approach to the interpretation of chest CT.

2
00:00:13,280 --> 00:00:21,500
Following completion of both modules, you should be able to employ a systematic search pattern for interpreting chest CTs.

3
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,380
It is important to have a thorough and systematic approach to interpreting a chest CT.

4
00:00:27,380 --> 00:00:43,520
This includes evaluation of the lymph nodes, the mediastinal structures, including the heart and pericardium, the pleura, the lungs and airways, including the trachea and major central airways, and finally, the bones and body wall.

5
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:51,710
This module will cover assessment of the lymph nodes and mediastinum, including the heart and pericardium.

6
00:00:52,350 --> 00:00:57,930
An example, normal dictation with pertinent negatives may read like this.

7
00:00:57,930 --> 00:01:10,510
There are no enlarged or suspicious intrathoracic, axillary, or image supraclavicular lymph nodes, no abnormal mediastinal mass, normal heart size without pericardial effusion or thickening.

8
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:24,840
Lymph node evaluation includes not only evaluation of the intrathoracic lymph nodes, but also the lymph nodes outside the thorax, within the axilla and the imaged lower neck.

9
00:01:24,840 --> 00:01:35,740
Remember, as you look for lymph nodes, you need to assess not only the lymph node size, but also for abnormalities in morphology, attenuation, and enhancement.

10
00:01:35,740 --> 00:01:46,700
Evaluation of intrathoracic lymph nodes includes assessment of the hylar lymph nodes bilaterally, as well as the mediastinal lymph nodes.

11
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:51,840
For example, the image on the left shows an enlarged right hylar lymph node.

12
00:01:52,580 --> 00:01:57,420
The image on the right shows an enlarged subchorinal lymph node.

13
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:08,080
Please review the nomenclature on mediastinal lymph nodes from our prior CT chest anatomy modules, and review your Anki note cards.

14
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:15,430
One location in which lymphadenopathy is often missed is the internal mammary station.

15
00:02:16,270 --> 00:02:20,190
Remember that the internal mammary vessels run along both sides of the sternum.

16
00:02:20,190 --> 00:02:25,290
Typically, there should be no identifiable lymph nodes along these vessels.

17
00:02:26,050 --> 00:02:32,570
However, in the example shown here, you can identify an enlarged right internal mammary lymph node.

18
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:45,700
Extrathoracic lymph nodes include axillary and subpectoral lymph nodes, as well as nodes in the imaged lower neck, above the capical, and along the scalene muscles.

19
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:54,100
The image on the right shows an enlarged supraclavicular lymph node with extension at and below the level of the left clavicle.

20
00:02:54,680 --> 00:02:59,880
The image on the left shows an enlarged right axillary lymph node.

21
00:03:01,960 --> 00:03:09,840
Remember that lymph nodes can be abnormal in both size and morphology, as well as enhancement and attenuation.

22
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:22,640
The general rule of thumb for size of lymph nodes, although there are exceptions to this rule, is about one centimeter in short axis dimension, so anything more than this you may consider suspicious.

23
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:28,580
These are two examples of abnormal lymph nodes, both in terms of their size and attenuation.

24
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:44,840
The image on the left shows a heterogeneous mediastinal lymph node with necrosis, while the image on the right shows a right paratracheal lymph node with thick peripheral calcification.

25
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:53,340
I typically do my search of intrathoracic lymph nodes as part of a comprehensive evaluation of the mediastinum.

26
00:03:53,340 --> 00:04:03,260
This search pattern includes assessment of overall mediastinal attenuation, including a search for the presence of gas.

27
00:04:04,620 --> 00:04:12,600
Evaluation of masses that should not be present, as well as those that should be present, including assessment of the vessels and heart.

28
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:22,420
Evaluation of the vascular structures should include an assessment of the size of the vessel or heart chamber.

29
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:29,920
The contour, the contour, patency, and evaluation for possible filling defects within these structures.

30
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:35,640
This is an example of abnormal attenuation of the mediastinum.

31
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:41,220
Specifically, there is gas scattered throughout the mediastinum, shown here.

32
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:43,620
This is termed pneumomediastinum.

33
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:52,080
Note also that this gas extends outside the thorax into the chest wall bilaterally, including in the left chest wall, as shown here.

34
00:04:52,420 --> 00:05:01,880
I typically make three passes through the mediastinum, evaluating each individual compartment with each pass.

35
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:07,340
Take a minute to review the compartmental model of the mediastinum that was described in a previous module.

36
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:16,060
Identifying the compartment in which a mass arises within the mediastinum can aid in differential diagnosis.

37
00:05:16,060 --> 00:05:23,100
Anterior mediastinal masses should displace middle mediastinal structures posteriorly.

38
00:05:23,440 --> 00:05:25,080
Here are two examples.

39
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:33,500
The image on the left shows a thymoma displacing the great vessels posteriorly.

40
00:05:34,340 --> 00:05:45,480
The image in the middle and on the right shows a thyroid mass extending into the mediastinum from the lower neck.

41
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:53,180
Note that there is displacement of the middle mediastinal structures on the axial image.

42
00:05:53,880 --> 00:05:57,800
The coronal image nicely shows the tracheal deviation as well.

43
00:06:00,410 --> 00:06:10,650
Middle mediastinal masses should displace posterior mediastinal structures posteriorly and anterior mediastinal structures anteriorly.

44
00:06:10,650 --> 00:06:15,110
In addition, they may surround middle mediastinal structures.

45
00:06:15,670 --> 00:06:17,010
Examples are shown here.

46
00:06:17,650 --> 00:06:31,010
On the image on the left, there is a mass which displaces the superior vena cava anteriorly as well as the esophagus, which is a posterior mediastinal structure, posteriorly.

47
00:06:31,010 --> 00:06:41,630
The image on the right shows an infiltrative mass surrounding a middle mediastinal structure, specifically the left pulmonary artery.

48
00:06:42,230 --> 00:06:50,950
The mass is located anterior to the descending thoracic aorta, which is a posterior mediastinal structure.

49
00:06:50,950 --> 00:07:04,110
Posterior mediastinal masses may be located posterior to a posterior mediastinal structure and or displace a middle mediastinal structure anteriorly.

50
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:20,990
The image on the left shows a mass in the left posterior mediastinum, which can be localized by realizing that this mass lies posterior to the descending thoracic aorta, which itself is a posterior mediastinal structure.

51
00:07:20,990 --> 00:07:25,050
The image on the right shows a dilated esophagus.

52
00:07:25,270 --> 00:07:28,170
Remember that the esophagus is in the posterior mediastinum.

53
00:07:28,730 --> 00:07:38,290
Note that the posterior mediastinal mass, in this case the dilated esophagus, displaces the right-sided airways anteriorly.

54
00:07:38,390 --> 00:07:42,490
And remember that the airways are a middle mediastinal structure.

55
00:07:42,490 --> 00:07:54,120
In addition to searching for mediastinal masses that shouldn't be present, you need to search for abnormalities of normal mediastinal structures, including the vessels.

56
00:07:54,960 --> 00:08:03,000
This search should include assessment of the size, patency, and contour of the vessel, as well as for intraluminal filling defects.

57
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:10,120
The image on the left shows enlargement of the descending thoracic aorta, consistent with an aortic aneurysm.

58
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:12,860
So this is an abnormality in vessel size.

59
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:28,580
The image on the right shows a small filling defect and contour abnormality of the proximal descending thoracic aorta, consistent with a pseudoaneurysm, secondary to blunt abdominal trauma.

60
00:08:28,580 --> 00:08:47,280
Types of filling defects that you may encounter include aethylosclerotic plaque, both calcified and non-calcified, thrombus, embolus, or an intomomedial flap in an aortic dissection, as shown here.

61
00:08:47,280 --> 00:08:59,440
The image on the left shows an aortic dissection involving both the ascending and descending thoracic aorta, as shown here.

62
00:08:59,960 --> 00:09:07,940
Any involvement of the ascending aorta is considered a type A aortic dissection and is a surgical case.

63
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:15,080
Notice here how that intomomedial flap shows up as a curvy linear filling defect within the aortic lumen.

64
00:09:15,580 --> 00:09:20,360
The image on the right shows a type B aortic dissection.

65
00:09:21,040 --> 00:09:27,180
In other words, this dissection involves the descending thoracic aorta only.

66
00:09:27,380 --> 00:09:31,360
There is no involvement of the ascending thoracic aorta.

67
00:09:31,360 --> 00:09:39,140
This is an example of an intomomedial flap, which creates a filling defect within the arterial lumen.

68
00:09:40,380 --> 00:09:45,320
Another example of a vascular filling defect is shown here, that is, an embolus.

69
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:52,620
These are examples of pulmonary emboli at different levels within the pulmonary arterial bed.

70
00:09:52,620 --> 00:10:01,820
The image on the left shows a filling defect from an embolus in the distal left pulmonary artery.

71
00:10:02,240 --> 00:10:08,560
The image in the middle shows a thin filling defect at the bifurcation of the main pulmonary artery,

72
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:12,320
with a larger filling defect more distally in the right pulmonary artery.

73
00:10:12,320 --> 00:10:28,020
The image on the right shows a central thrombus within the segmental and subsegmental branches of the right lower lobe pulmonary arterial bed.

74
00:10:35,620 --> 00:10:39,560
This is a case of achalasia with marked esophageal dilatation.

75
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:45,740
Note that there is anterior displacement of the trachea, which is a middle mediastinal structure.

76
00:10:45,740 --> 00:10:53,320
So this is a dilated esophagus, and remember that the esophagus is located in the posterior mediastinum.

77
00:10:55,920 --> 00:11:00,520
When evaluating the heart, it is important to assess globally for heart size,

78
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:03,900
that is, for overall enlargement or overall cardiomegaly,

79
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:08,760
in addition to evaluating for specific chamber enlargement.

80
00:11:09,400 --> 00:11:14,760
You need to assess the chamber contours, as well as for intraluminal filling defects.

81
00:11:14,760 --> 00:11:20,240
And then finally, you want to evaluate for calcifications in and around the heart,

82
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:27,080
including calcifications in the coronary artery, the valve leaflets and annulus, as well as the myocardium.

83
00:11:28,260 --> 00:11:34,260
Here are some common examples of chamber enlargement that you will see in clinical practice.

84
00:11:34,260 --> 00:11:40,420
The image on the left shows overall heart enlargement, consistent with global cardiomegaly.

85
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:45,500
The middle image shows enlargement of the left atrium.

86
00:11:46,360 --> 00:11:49,700
One rule of thumb is anything more than 4 centimeters.

87
00:11:51,140 --> 00:11:58,920
In terms of the anteroposterior length or thickness of the left atrium,

88
00:11:58,920 --> 00:12:03,040
on an axial image should be considered potentially enlarged.

89
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:08,300
Also, note here that there is enlargement of the left atrial appendage.

90
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:14,920
The image on the right shows right heart strain from an acute pulmonary embolus,

91
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:18,340
resulting in enlargement of the right ventricle,

92
00:12:18,820 --> 00:12:21,980
such that the right ventricle, as shown here,

93
00:12:21,980 --> 00:12:27,520
is actually larger than the left ventricle, secondary to the increased pressure.

94
00:12:27,700 --> 00:12:30,880
So the RV to LV ratio is greater than 1.

95
00:12:33,350 --> 00:12:38,710
Shown here are examples of a filling defect within the cardiac chambers.

96
00:12:39,630 --> 00:12:47,110
Secondary to thrombus, the image on the left shows a small thrombus at the left ventricular apex,

97
00:12:47,110 --> 00:12:53,030
while the image on the right shows a filling defect from thrombus in the right heart.

98
00:12:55,250 --> 00:13:00,910
Calcifications involving the heart and its associated structures include coronary artery calcification,

99
00:13:01,590 --> 00:13:02,490
as shown here.

100
00:13:03,750 --> 00:13:11,130
Calcification of the aortic valves can include both calcification of the valve leaflets,

101
00:13:11,510 --> 00:13:12,930
as shown on the aortic valve,

102
00:13:12,930 --> 00:13:18,990
as well as the valve annulus, as shown here, involving the mitral valve.

103
00:13:21,260 --> 00:13:25,340
Your search should also include evaluation of the pericardium.

104
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:30,200
Specifically, you should assess for pericardial thickening or mass,

105
00:13:30,720 --> 00:13:35,800
as well as for the accumulation of material within the pericardial space itself.

106
00:13:36,360 --> 00:13:39,500
This includes fluid, termed a pericardial effusion.

107
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:45,200
Remember that effusions can be both simple or complex, depending on their etiology.

108
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,240
Gas can also accumulate within the pericardial space,

109
00:13:50,720 --> 00:13:52,940
and that's termed pneumopericardium.

110
00:13:54,900 --> 00:13:56,680
In the example shown here,

111
00:13:57,260 --> 00:14:02,120
note the gas attenuation surrounding the heart in the pericardial space.

112
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:05,100
This is the heart itself on lung windows.

113
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:10,620
Notice the gas in the pericardial cavity that surrounds the heart.

114
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:12,360
This is pneumopericardium.

115
00:14:13,460 --> 00:14:20,240
This is a coronal CT illustrating fluid accumulating within the pericardial space.

116
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:22,220
That is a pericardial effusion.

117
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:28,140
Note that the pericardium does extend up to the level of the great vessels,

118
00:14:28,260 --> 00:14:29,880
and this can sometimes be confusing,

119
00:14:29,880 --> 00:14:37,120
particularly on axial images at the level of the great vessels where you see fluid in the pericardial recesses,

120
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:40,680
and you think, oh, maybe that's too high for the actual pericardium.

121
00:14:40,800 --> 00:14:46,880
So just be aware that that pericardium extends up to the level of the great vessels.

122
00:14:47,540 --> 00:14:49,860
Here we can see the heart on the coronal image,

123
00:14:49,860 --> 00:14:53,060
the fat surrounding the heart,

124
00:14:53,860 --> 00:14:58,720
and then finally the fluid in the pericardial space,

125
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:01,020
superficial to that fat,

126
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:07,000
extending all the way up along the reflections onto the great vessels.

127
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:12,400
Here are examples of pericardial effusions.

128
00:15:12,960 --> 00:15:17,960
The image on the left shows a simple pericardial effusion as shown here.

129
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:22,460
That is, the fluid measures simple fluid attenuation,

130
00:15:22,680 --> 00:15:28,120
typically somewhere between negative 10 Hounsfield units and positive 20 Hounsfield units.

131
00:15:28,520 --> 00:15:30,120
Think of this as water.

132
00:15:30,940 --> 00:15:35,160
And this type of effusion is what would be associated with a transudative process.

133
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:41,960
The image on the right, however, shows fluid that measures higher than simple fluid attenuation,

134
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:48,100
suggesting an exudative process, such as infection, hemorrhage, or malignancy.

135
00:15:48,780 --> 00:15:52,660
Now, in addition to evaluating the fluid itself,

136
00:15:53,720 --> 00:15:59,160
you have to assess the pericardium for any abnormal thickening or enhancement.

137
00:15:59,780 --> 00:16:01,400
On the image on the left,

138
00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:05,300
notice that the pericardium is not only thick and enhancing,

139
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:11,460
but also has nodular components consistent with underlying malignancy.

140
00:16:11,620 --> 00:16:15,780
So this is an example of a malignant pericardial effusion.

141
00:16:16,260 --> 00:16:29,650
This image shows multiple calcifications located within the arteries surrounding the heart,

142
00:16:29,810 --> 00:16:31,250
including the ascending aorta,

143
00:16:32,170 --> 00:16:33,850
the left coronary artery,

144
00:16:33,850 --> 00:16:41,930
and then the calcification in question was actually a calcification of the circumflex coronary artery.

145
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:49,360
As in prior modules regarding an approach to interpretation of a radiographic study,

146
00:16:49,780 --> 00:16:54,600
here we have discussed the importance of using a thorough and systematic search pattern

147
00:16:54,600 --> 00:16:56,820
for evaluation of chest CTs.

148
00:16:57,320 --> 00:17:01,980
In this module, I have discussed assessment of the lymph nodes and mediastinum

149
00:17:01,980 --> 00:17:05,060
to include evaluation of the heart and pericardium.

150
00:17:06,100 --> 00:17:09,380
Remember that lymph nodes can be abnormal not only in size,

151
00:17:09,500 --> 00:17:13,460
but also in morphology and attenuation and enhancement.

152
00:17:14,360 --> 00:17:18,160
Not only do we need to evaluate for intrathoracic lymph nodes,

153
00:17:18,220 --> 00:17:20,660
that is in the hyla and mediastinum,

154
00:17:20,880 --> 00:17:24,360
but we also have to assess the axillary region

155
00:17:24,360 --> 00:17:28,000
and the imaged lower portions of the neck.

156
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:34,340
In the mediastinum, we need to evaluate for structures or masses that shouldn't be there.

157
00:17:34,840 --> 00:17:37,720
If we identify an abnormal mass in the mediastinum,

158
00:17:37,880 --> 00:17:40,660
it's important to localize it to a compartment

159
00:17:40,660 --> 00:17:45,540
as this will help us generate a differential diagnosis for the etiology of the mass.

160
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:51,360
We also need to evaluate structures that are normally in the mediastinum,

161
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:59,480
and particularly the vessels and heart for things like size, patency, contour deformities, and filling defects.

162
00:18:00,760 --> 00:18:06,460
Finally, we need assessment of not only the heart, but also the pericardium,

163
00:18:06,920 --> 00:18:11,280
including searching for abnormal pericardial thickening or mass,

164
00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:15,720
as well as collection of material within the pericardial space,

165
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:19,920
including pericardial effusions and pneumopericardium.

166
00:18:20,820 --> 00:18:22,460
That's the end of this unit.

167
00:18:22,880 --> 00:18:27,800
Please complete the corresponding quiz questions to receive credit for this course.

